1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,566
A doctor checks your temperature.

2
00:00:02,566 --> 00:00:04,239
A shopkeeper weighs rice.

3
00:00:04,239 --> 00:00:05,672
You measure a table.

4
00:00:06,072 --> 00:00:08,013
Three very different jobs.

5
00:00:08,013 --> 00:00:10,865
But all three use the same basic idea.

6
00:00:11,315 --> 00:00:13,487
Even Diya's recipe card needs it.

7
00:00:13,487 --> 00:00:15,147
It says: sugar, two.

8
00:00:15,497 --> 00:00:16,461
Two spoons?

9
00:00:16,461 --> 00:00:17,361
Two cups?

10
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:18,442
Two kilograms?

11
00:00:18,442 --> 00:00:20,643
The number alone means nothing.

12
00:00:21,143 --> 00:00:23,942
By the end of this
video, you will see why.

13
00:00:23,942 --> 00:00:26,226
It is the first big idea in physics.

14
00:00:26,876 --> 00:00:28,954
So what is a physical quantity?

15
00:00:29,304 --> 00:00:32,027
It is anything in nature
that we can measure.

16
00:00:32,027 --> 00:00:34,625
Length, mass, time, temperature, speed.

17
00:00:34,975 --> 00:00:38,678
Kindness and beauty matter, but
no instrument can measure them.

18
00:00:38,678 --> 00:00:40,854
So they are not physical quantities.

19
00:00:41,454 --> 00:00:43,492
Every measurement has two parts.

20
00:00:43,492 --> 00:00:45,576
A number, called the magnitude.

21
00:00:45,576 --> 00:00:46,505
And a unit.

22
00:00:46,855 --> 00:00:51,826
A unit is a fixed amount we all agree
on, like one metre or one kilogram.

23
00:00:52,476 --> 00:00:55,270
Let's measure one pencil
on our measuring bench.

24
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:58,470
With a centimetre scale, it reads fifteen.

25
00:00:58,470 --> 00:00:59,831
Fifteen centimetres.

26
00:01:00,231 --> 00:01:01,651
Switch to millimetres.

27
00:01:01,651 --> 00:01:03,897
Now it reads one hundred and fifty.

28
00:01:04,297 --> 00:01:05,580
Switch to metres.

29
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:08,064
Now it is only zero point one five.

30
00:01:08,464 --> 00:01:10,012
The pencil never changed.

31
00:01:10,012 --> 00:01:12,431
The number and the unit changed together.

32
00:01:12,931 --> 00:01:14,218
Look at the graph.

33
00:01:14,218 --> 00:01:17,207
Unit ten times bigger,
number ten times smaller.

34
00:01:17,857 --> 00:01:20,682
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

35
00:01:21,032 --> 00:01:21,993
Step one.

36
00:01:21,993 --> 00:01:26,168
A quantity Q equals its
number n, times its unit u.

37
00:01:26,168 --> 00:01:27,505
Q equals n u.

38
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:29,155
Step two.

39
00:01:29,142 --> 00:01:33,145
The same quantity in two
different units does not change.

40
00:01:33,145 --> 00:01:35,904
So n one u one equals n two u two.

41
00:01:36,654 --> 00:01:37,567
Step three.

42
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:41,407
n times u is fixed, so n is
inversely proportional to u.

43
00:01:41,407 --> 00:01:43,908
A bigger unit means a smaller number.

44
00:01:44,658 --> 00:01:45,682
Remember this.

45
00:01:45,682 --> 00:01:50,313
A physical quantity is always a number
with a unit, never a number alone.

46
00:01:51,013 --> 00:01:54,236
Now, physicists sort
quantities in two big ways.

47
00:01:54,586 --> 00:01:57,904
The first way: base quantities
and derived quantities.

48
00:01:58,254 --> 00:02:00,615
Base quantities are the building blocks.

49
00:02:00,615 --> 00:02:02,759
The SI system picks seven of them.

50
00:02:03,109 --> 00:02:07,607
Length, mass, time, electric current,
temperature, amount of substance,

51
00:02:07,607 --> 00:02:09,106
and luminous intensity.

52
00:02:09,506 --> 00:02:12,200
Derived quantities are built from these.

53
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:13,149
Take speed.

54
00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:16,346
Speed equals distance divided by time.

55
00:02:16,746 --> 00:02:19,866
Distance is a length, so
it is a base quantity.

56
00:02:19,866 --> 00:02:21,813
Time is also a base quantity.

57
00:02:22,213 --> 00:02:24,952
So speed is made from two base quantities.

58
00:02:24,952 --> 00:02:26,946
That is why speed is derived.

59
00:02:27,346 --> 00:02:28,399
Side by side.

60
00:02:28,399 --> 00:02:29,550
Length is base.

61
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,812
You read it straight off a ruler.

62
00:02:32,212 --> 00:02:33,366
Speed is derived.

63
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:36,530
You need a length and a
time, and then you divide.

64
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:38,170
Now you try.

65
00:02:38,170 --> 00:02:41,643
Is temperature a base quantity
or a derived quantity?

66
00:02:41,643 --> 00:02:43,561
Pause the video and decide.

67
00:02:46,761 --> 00:02:47,661
Base!

68
00:02:47,293 --> 00:02:49,390
Temperature is one of the seven.

69
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:53,688
A thermometer reads it directly, and
it is not built from length, mass

70
00:02:53,688 --> 00:02:54,588
or time.

71
00:02:55,069 --> 00:02:57,386
The second way: scalars and vectors.

72
00:02:57,736 --> 00:02:59,778
A scalar has only a size.

73
00:02:59,778 --> 00:03:03,105
Five kilograms of rice
is just five kilograms.

74
00:03:03,105 --> 00:03:04,676
No direction needed.

75
00:03:05,026 --> 00:03:07,539
A vector has a size and a direction.

76
00:03:07,539 --> 00:03:10,479
Displacement, velocity
and force are vectors.

77
00:03:10,879 --> 00:03:13,389
Push a box with ten newtons to the right.

78
00:03:13,389 --> 00:03:14,433
It moves right.

79
00:03:14,783 --> 00:03:18,783
Push with the same ten newtons
to the left, and it moves left.

80
00:03:18,783 --> 00:03:20,665
Same size, different result.

81
00:03:20,665 --> 00:03:22,462
So force needs a direction.

82
00:03:23,112 --> 00:03:24,048
Your turn.

83
00:03:24,048 --> 00:03:26,545
Is velocity a scalar or a vector?

84
00:03:26,545 --> 00:03:27,923
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:31,123 --> 00:03:32,023
A vector!

86
00:03:31,971 --> 00:03:34,681
Velocity is speed in a given direction.

87
00:03:34,681 --> 00:03:38,942
Fifty kilometres per hour north
is not the same as fifty south.

88
00:03:39,592 --> 00:03:41,863
Let's sort six quantities on the board.

89
00:03:42,213 --> 00:03:44,532
Mass and time are base, and scalar.

90
00:03:44,882 --> 00:03:47,591
Speed and density are derived, and scalar.

91
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:51,315
Velocity and force are
derived, and they are vectors.

92
00:03:51,665 --> 00:03:53,569
Notice one box stays empty.

93
00:03:53,569 --> 00:03:56,664
None of the seven base
quantities is a vector.

94
00:03:57,314 --> 00:03:59,050
Now, one worked example.

95
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:02,254
A steel rod is two point five metres long.

96
00:04:02,254 --> 00:04:04,338
How long is it in centimetres?

97
00:04:04,838 --> 00:04:05,967
Use the rule.

98
00:04:05,967 --> 00:04:09,645
n two equals n one times
u one, divided by u two.

99
00:04:10,395 --> 00:04:12,414
Here n one is two point five.

100
00:04:12,414 --> 00:04:13,843
u one is one metre.

101
00:04:13,843 --> 00:04:15,510
u two is one centimetre.

102
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:19,018
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

103
00:04:19,018 --> 00:04:22,495
So n two is two point
five times one hundred.

104
00:04:23,245 --> 00:04:25,195
That is two hundred and fifty.

105
00:04:25,195 --> 00:04:28,236
The rod is two hundred and
fifty centimetres long.

106
00:04:28,886 --> 00:04:30,351
Now, the exam trap.

107
00:04:30,701 --> 00:04:35,623
Many students say electric current is a
vector, because it flows in a direction.

108
00:04:35,973 --> 00:04:37,668
But current is a scalar.

109
00:04:37,668 --> 00:04:41,998
At a junction, two amperes and three
amperes always make five amperes,

110
00:04:41,998 --> 00:04:43,335
whatever the angle.

111
00:04:43,335 --> 00:04:45,330
Vectors do not add like that.

112
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:47,343
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:47,343 --> 00:04:51,669
As quick one mark questions: spot the
base quantity, the derived one, or

114
00:04:51,669 --> 00:04:52,569
the vector.

115
00:04:53,111 --> 00:04:54,394
Exam level now.

116
00:04:54,394 --> 00:04:58,580
Which set has only derived
quantities that are also scalars?

117
00:04:58,580 --> 00:04:59,917
Pause and solve.

118
00:05:05,317 --> 00:05:06,661
The answer is A.

119
00:05:06,661 --> 00:05:08,526
Speed, density and work.

120
00:05:09,026 --> 00:05:11,414
B has mass and time, both base.

121
00:05:11,414 --> 00:05:13,216
C has force, a vector.

122
00:05:13,716 --> 00:05:16,499
D has current and temperature, both base.

123
00:05:16,499 --> 00:05:19,243
Only A is fully derived and fully scalar.

124
00:05:19,843 --> 00:05:21,302
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:21,302 --> 00:05:22,802
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:26,002 --> 00:05:29,000
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:30,300
One.

128
00:05:29,871 --> 00:05:32,854
Why is sugar, two, not
a complete measurement?

129
00:05:33,754 --> 00:05:34,654
Two.

130
00:05:34,268 --> 00:05:36,495
Why is speed a derived quantity?

131
00:05:37,395 --> 00:05:38,295
Three.

132
00:05:37,996 --> 00:05:41,394
Is electric current a
scalar or a vector, and why?

133
00:05:42,494 --> 00:05:46,906
Next time: if speed is built from
length and time, what is force built

134
00:05:46,906 --> 00:05:47,806
from?

135
00:05:47,445 --> 00:05:50,550
We find out in fundamental
and derived quantities.
